## Change — fpq-orm-semantics (ws/orm-semantics)

| run | driver tuples | stock ORM | materialize e2e | materialize only | materialize fallback |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | 119.6 ns/row (8.36M) | 3605.2 ns/row (0.28M) | 475.7 ns/row (2.10M) | 160.9 ns/row (6.22M) | 3816.6 ns/row (0.26M) |
| 2 | 119.0 ns/row (8.40M) | 3568.7 ns/row (0.28M) | 470.8 ns/row (2.12M) | 161.1 ns/row (6.21M) | 3793.3 ns/row (0.26M) |
| 3 | 114.4 ns/row (8.74M) | 3585.2 ns/row (0.28M) | 476.2 ns/row (2.10M) | 164.7 ns/row (6.07M) | 3789.9 ns/row (0.26M) |
| **median** | **119.0 ns/row (8.40M)** | **3585.2 ns/row (0.28M)** | **475.7 ns/row (2.10M)** | **161.1 ns/row (6.21M)** | **3793.3 ns/row (0.26M)** |

```
### ORM MATERIALIZE
rows: 100000  reps: 5  shape: bench
engine: postgresql+fastpostgres://postgres@/postgres?host=/tmp
driver tuples            119.0 ns/row     8.40M rows/s
stock ORM               3585.2 ns/row     0.28M rows/s
materialize e2e          475.7 ns/row     2.10M rows/s
materialize only         161.1 ns/row     6.21M rows/s
materialize fallback    3793.3 ns/row     0.26M rows/s
speedup e2e:        7.54x  entity-only: 22.25x
### END ORM MATERIALIZE
```

`materialize only` is the fill on the driver's rows after the first warm call
has decoded the lazy `ResultSet` (its `_materialized` cache); `e2e` includes
the read and the decode. `materialize fallback` uses a model with a
relationship, so it pays for a real `InstanceState` and an identity key; it is
reported as the price of correctness, not as a speedup.

## ### AB

- **Baseline stock ORM 3373.4 ns/row (0.30M rows/s) -> materialize e2e 475.7
  ns/row (2.10M rows/s): -85.9% ns/row, 7.09x** (0.296M -> 2.102M rows/s
  before rounding). Against the same image's stock ORM control (3585.2 ns/row),
  e2e is -86.7% and 7.54x.
- The fill alone is **161.1 ns/row (6.21M rows/s)**, 22.25x the same image's
  stock ORM row; it is 20.9x the baseline stock ORM row.
- The fallback is **3793.3 ns/row (0.26M rows/s)**, +5.8% against this image's
  stock ORM control and +12.4% against the baseline stock ORM (3373.4). That is
  the intended contract: models the fast path cannot honor get stock objects,
  not a slower half-ORM.
- Controls moved between images: driver tuples 213.6 -> 119.0 ns/row (-44.3%,
  1.80x) because `merge/read-path` carries the transport work; this change does
  not touch the transport. Stock ORM moved 3373.4 -> 3585.2 ns/row (+6.3%),
  which is the drift to read the materializer speedups against.
- The merged `ResultSet` is lazy; `materialize` now decodes it once through
  `to_tuples()` (C frame decode) instead of letting `PySequence_Fast` walk its
  Python iterator into a throwaway list. Before that decode was added, the same
  image measured e2e 557.0 ns/row and fill 425.2 ns/row; this final run
  measures 475.7 and 161.1. This is consumer-side only.

## Tests

`flock /tmp/fpq-bench.lock -c 'docker run --rm --shm-size=3g fpq-orm-semantics tests.sh'`

```
### extension: make installcheck
# All 1 tests passed.
### client: unittest
Ran 86 tests in 1.492s
OK (skipped=1)
```

The ORM suite is 20 tests: fast-path values/order, no `_sa_instance_state`,
`inspect()` raising, repr/identity, defaults not applied on read, empty lazy
`ResultSet`, and boundary tests for relationships, validators, deferred
columns, versioning, composites, synonyms, polymorphism (including subclass
restoration from the discriminator) and raiseload, plus partial projections
falling back to stock instances.
